# Class instance references in class files

**Allowed:** intentional logical singletons that are part of the design (global service objects). Example: `LOGGER`.

**Not allowed:** declaring a pointer/reference to “the current instance” inside that class’s own `.h` / `.cpp` just so a static/C callback can reach `this` (ad-hoc `static Foo *instance = this`).

```cpp
// BAD — fake singleton only to serve a library callback
class MqttClient {
    static MqttClient *instance;
};
MqttClient *MqttClient::instance = nullptr;
instance = this;
```

Prefer wiring from outside the class (e.g. `main.cpp`): register a free/static callback or handler that owns/holds the needed state, or pass user-data context when the library supports it.

```cpp
// GOOD — ownership and callback wiring live outside the class implementation
mqtt = new MqttClient(settings);
mqtt->setPumpControlHandler(onMqttPumpControl);
```

```cpp
// GOOD — logical singleton by design
Logger LOGGER;
```
